1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,974
Liquid templates can live inside your content.

2
00:00:03,974 --> 00:00:08,036
The Liquid part and the Liquid field store a template, and run it

3
00:00:08,036 --> 00:00:10,324
whenever the content is displayed.

4
00:00:10,324 --> 00:00:14,900
In this video, you add each one, write a template, and see how it renders.

5
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:24,520
The Liquid module provides two building blocks for content types that run templates.

6
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:27,420
The Liquid part is the body of a content item.

7
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:32,050
The Liquid field is a named block inside a part, and a part can have several.

8
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:38,456
Their source is stored exactly as you submit it, and runs when the content is displayed.

9
00:00:38,456 --> 00:00:42,788
The HTML body part, the HTML field, and the Markdown body part and

10
00:00:42,788 --> 00:00:44,594
field do not run Liquid.

11
00:00:44,594 --> 00:00:46,820
They treat it as ordinary text.

12
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:56,058
Let's add the Liquid part to a content type named Notice, which has a Title part.

13
00:00:56,058 --> 00:00:59,860
On the Edit Content Type page, under Parts, choose Add Parts.

14
00:01:00,342 --> 00:01:03,800
The part is listed as Liquid, with the description:

15
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:07,500
Provides a Liquid formatted body for your content item.

16
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,352
Select it, and choose Add Selected Parts.

17
00:01:10,831 --> 00:01:13,822
In Content Items, choose New, then Notice.

18
00:01:13,822 --> 00:01:15,068
Enter a title.

19
00:01:15,068 --> 00:01:19,118
The Liquid part adds an editor labeled Body, with the hint:

20
00:01:19,118 --> 00:01:22,421
The body of the content item, in Liquid format.

21
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:24,209
Write the template.

22
00:01:24,209 --> 00:01:27,915
It prints the site name from the Site object, and the modified date

23
00:01:27,915 --> 00:01:32,230
of the ContentItem, converted to the time zone of the site by the local filter.

24
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:34,950
Liquid is checked when you save.

25
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:38,221
Add an if tag with no end tag and choose Publish:

26
00:01:38,221 --> 00:01:42,741
the item is not saved, and the message begins, The Liquid Body doesn't

27
00:01:42,741 --> 00:01:45,120
contain a valid Liquid expression.

28
00:01:45,675 --> 00:01:49,024
Correct the template and publish again, then choose View.

29
00:01:49,024 --> 00:01:52,692
The page shows the title in the header, and below it the output

30
00:01:52,692 --> 00:01:55,615
of the template, with the site name and the date.

31
00:01:59,852 --> 00:02:01,878
Next comes the Liquid field.

32
00:02:01,878 --> 00:02:05,928
On the Edit Content Type page, under Fields, choose Add Field.

33
00:02:05,928 --> 00:02:10,752
Enter Closing Note as the Display Name; the Technical Name follows from it.

34
00:02:11,244 --> 00:02:13,950
Choose the field type Liquid Field, and save.

35
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:17,664
The field is added to the part named after the type, which holds

36
00:02:17,664 --> 00:02:19,574
the fields of the type itself.

37
00:02:20,031 --> 00:02:21,325
Edit the notice.

38
00:02:21,325 --> 00:02:25,851
The editor is labeled with the display name of the field, Closing Note,

39
00:02:25,851 --> 00:02:27,321
and its hint reads:

40
00:02:27,321 --> 00:02:30,201
The content of the field, in Liquid format.

41
00:02:30,201 --> 00:02:34,551
Write a template that links to the item with the display URL filter.

42
00:02:35,008 --> 00:02:37,188
Publish, then view the item again.

43
00:02:37,188 --> 00:02:39,859
The output of the field is on the page too:

44
00:02:39,859 --> 00:02:41,658
a link to the content item.

45
00:02:45,858 --> 00:02:48,098
A migration does the same from code.

46
00:02:48,098 --> 00:02:52,044
WithPart attaches the Liquid part by name, and the field goes on the

47
00:02:52,044 --> 00:02:55,458
part named after the type, with OfType set to LiquidField.

48
00:02:59,658 --> 00:03:02,569
Orchard Core renders content through shapes.

49
00:03:02,569 --> 00:03:08,857
The module describes the shapes LiquidPart, LiquidPart_Summary and LiquidField, and renders the stored

50
00:03:08,857 --> 00:03:11,128
source when a shape is processed.

51
00:03:11,878 --> 00:03:17,266
The shape itself is the Model, and the content item is passed in as ContentItem.

52
00:03:17,266 --> 00:03:19,396
The result is not sanitized:

53
00:03:19,396 --> 00:03:24,158
it becomes the Html property of the shape, and the shape's view writes

54
00:03:24,158 --> 00:03:26,038
it to the page as it is.

55
00:03:26,788 --> 00:03:32,095
The summary view removes the tags and cuts the text to about 200 characters.

56
00:03:32,095 --> 00:03:37,468
The field view wraps the Html in a div with the class field-type-liquidfield.

57
00:03:38,218 --> 00:03:40,652
Other code reads the same sources.

58
00:03:40,652 --> 00:03:45,277
A feed asks for the body aspect, which holds the rendered Liquid part.

59
00:03:45,277 --> 00:03:51,058
And in GraphQL, a Liquid field has a field named liquid that returns the rendered result.

60
00:03:55,258 --> 00:03:59,339
Editing Liquid takes its own permission, Manage Liquid templates.

61
00:03:59,339 --> 00:04:04,858
It is marked security-critical, and the Administrator and Editor roles have it by default.

62
00:04:05,608 --> 00:04:10,082
Without it, a user cannot edit or publish a content item that has

63
00:04:10,082 --> 00:04:15,188
a Liquid part or a Liquid field, even one nested inside a Flow or Bag part.

64
00:04:17,188 --> 00:04:19,662
That's the Liquid part and the Liquid field:

65
00:04:19,662 --> 00:04:23,422
templates that live in your content, run when it is displayed, and are

66
00:04:23,422 --> 00:04:25,401
protected by their own permission.

67
00:04:25,401 --> 00:04:26,688
Thanks for watching.

